+**AI-CSI feedback vs eType-II codebooks**
+
+Rel-19 studied two-sided AI/ML CSI compression but deferred normative support to Rel-20. (a) Quantify the baseline: what does a high-resolution eType-II PMI report cost in UCI bits (order of magnitude) and why does MU-MIMO precoding quality depend on it. (b) Explain the two-sided architecture: what runs where, and why the 'pairing' problem between UE-side encoder and network-side decoder makes this substantially harder to standardize than a one-sided model. (c) State the gain axis the Rel-19 evaluations showed (bits at equal accuracy or accuracy at equal bits) and the metric used to monitor reconstruction quality. (d) Give the two lifecycle-management problems that drove the deferral, and the fallback any eventual deployment must keep.
+
+**Reference answer:**
+
+(a) eType-II reports (W1 beam selection + W2 per-subband amplitude/phase, high parameter combos) cost on the order of **several hundred UCI bits per report** (≈300-800+ depending on rank/paramCombination/subbands) — a large PUSCH/PUCCH overhead at short periodicities. MU-MIMO ZF/RZF precoding needs accurate per-subband channel/eigenvector knowledge to null inter-user leakage; quantization error directly becomes residual interference, so precoding gain is bounded by feedback resolution.
+(b) Two-sided: a **UE-side encoder** compresses the estimated channel/eigenvectors into a latent representation; a **network-side decoder** reconstructs for precoding. Encoder and decoder are trained jointly — so a UE-chipset encoder must be *paired* with each vendor's decoder. Multi-vendor reality: offline joint training across every UE-vendor x network-vendor pair, or standardized dataset/reference-model exchange; without a working pairing/interoperability regime, a UE meeting the spec could still be incompatible with a given RAN's decoder — this is the core standardization difficulty — and the principal reason Rel-19 concluded at study/evaluation level while normative two-sided support moved to Rel-20 (one-sided use cases carry no cross-vendor joint-training burden).
+(c) Gain axis: **fewer feedback bits at comparable CSI accuracy** (or better accuracy at equal bits) — order 2-4x UCI compression in evaluations; monitored via **SGCS** (squared generalized cosine similarity) between reconstructed and reference precoders/eigenvectors (NMSE as alternative), tracked as a monitoring KPI to detect model degradation.
+(d) LCM problems: (1) **performance monitoring & fallback decision** — detecting silently degraded reconstruction in the field (distribution shift by scenario/site) without ground truth at the gNB, and deciding when to switch models; (2) **model identity/versioning across the ecosystem** — activation, switching, transfer, and pairing-compatibility signalling for models the network didn't train (UE-side updates changing behavior). Mandatory fallback: **legacy codebook-based reporting (eType-II)** always retained and switchable per UE, so AI-CSI failure degrades to known performance, never below it.
+
+**Grading notes:** Must have: eType-II cost order (hundreds of bits) tied to MU-MIMO leakage, two-sided split + pairing as the standardization crux (correctly framed: studied in Rel-19, normative work in Rel-20), compression-at-equal-accuracy framing with SGCS (or NMSE) monitoring, both LCM problems, and codebook fallback. Accept differing bit counts within order of magnitude and either 'deferral' or 'phased standardization' framing. Penalize: claiming two-sided AI-CSI is already normatively standardized and deployable, one-sided-model descriptions of the compression use case, or no fallback story.
