+## TS3 · Timing & Sync · expert · 8 pts
+
+**Timing resilience beyond full on-path support**
+
+A TDD operator cannot get full on-path PTP support in leased metro segments, and is also worried about GNSS jamming/spoofing at cell-site receivers. Design the timing architecture: (a) what changes between G.8275.1 and G.8275.2 profiles and what APTS means operationally; (b) why leased dark-segment asymmetry is the dominant error source in partial support and how it is bounded; (c) a two-layer GNSS attack defense — detection signals and the holdover strategy with concrete class/duration expectations; (d) the network-side backstop that limits how long any site must free-run.
+
+**Reference answer:**
+
+(a) **G.8275.1** = full timing support, every node a T-BC/T-TC, PTP over L2 hop-by-hop; **G.8275.2** = partial timing support, PTP over IP through PTP-unaware segments (unicast), tolerating nodes that add uncorrected delay. **APTS** (assisted partial timing support): GNSS at the edge site is the primary reference and the PTP path runs as *assist/backup* — while GNSS is healthy the site calibrates the PTP path's asymmetry against GNSS, so on GNSS loss the PTP backup takes over with a pre-measured correction.
+(b) PTP measures one mean path delay and assumes symmetry; through unaware leased segments, **forward/reverse delay differ** (different fiber routes/lengths, congestion-dependent queuing per direction) and half the asymmetry lands directly as time error — µs-class if uncontrolled, dwarfing every other contributor. Bounded by: APTS-style **asymmetry calibration against GNSS**, procuring symmetric-routed circuits with SLAs, prioritized PTP marking to minimize queuing spread, and monitoring (comparing PTP-vs-GNSS offset trends to catch re-routes that change asymmetry).
+(c) Detection layer: GNSS receiver integrity — RAIM/multi-constellation cross-check, C/N0 and AGC anomaly monitoring (jamming signature), position/time jump detection at a *static* site (spoofing signature: a fixed antenna 'moving'), plus cross-source comparison against the PTP reference (sudden GNSS-vs-PTP divergence flags the compromised source). Holdover layer: on declared GNSS failure, switch to PTP (APTS) if healthy; if both are suspect, **oscillator holdover** — OCXO holds ~1.5 µs for hours-class, rubidium for a day-class, ePRTC-grade (cesium-assisted) for multi-day; the site should be engineered so the holdover class covers the expected repair/mitigation time for the site tier.
+(d) Backstop: a **resilient distributed PRTC architecture** — regional ePRTC/cesium-anchored grandmasters (and increasingly, sources independent of open-sky GNSS such as fiber-delivered ePRTC references) feeding the PTP network, so a GNSS event at a site (or regionally) degrades to 'PTP from an unaffected anchor', bounding free-run to the switchover time rather than the attack duration; TDD neighbors sharing a sync island also bound *relative* error, which is what interference actually cares about.
+
+**Grading notes:** Must have: 8275.1 vs .2 vs APTS operational distinction, asymmetry-as-dominant-error with calibration bounding, two-layer defense (integrity detection incl. spoofing-vs-jamming signatures; holdover classes with realistic durations), and network-anchor backstop with the relative-error insight. Oscillator holdover durations are illustrative — accept any figures consistent with the stated oscillator class and sound engineering; grade the strategy, not the constants. Penalize: 'GNSS everywhere' with no attack story, symmetric-path assumption unexamined, or holdover treated as unlimited.
